  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Factorization now follows Sage's commutative integral-power semantics: exponent one preserves identity, zero returns the neutral generic factorization, and negative powers negate formal exponents while exact polynomial factors and units enter their fraction field. Rational-function units remain closed under later signed powers, polynomial fraction fields reject composite-modulus coefficient rings, and unit formatting uses Sage-compatible parent atomicity metadata including RealField and mixed factors. Six focused differential regressions cover integer, rational, polynomial, and rational-function composition, display, value reconstruction, non-domain rejection, zero powers, and zero inversion failures.",
    "risks": [
      "Factorization currently models only the existing commutative Sage.js core; Sage's separate noncommutative inversion path remains outside this lane.",
      "Automatic fraction-field entry uses the existing polynomial _factorization_repr capability; other future element families must implement their own exact negative power before formal factors over them can be expanded.",
      "The deterministic changed-file run was attempted against the lane base and retained as a failed receipt: compiler/algebra.py exceeded its shared-load timeout but passed alone, while compiler/matrix.py failed in the unrelated sparse-random fixture. Focused, strict, portable, and the complete native gate pass on the final workspace fingerprint."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Integrate after independent review and retain the focused test in the arithmetic compatibility suite."
    ]
  }
